From 0774b8c81edde899a7ccf76b1e586fda13d262c3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jonathan Barnes <hello@jonbarnes.dev>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 01:12:36 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Acceleration structures from the frame, and the primary ray
 that sees them

Patch 0003 of the renderer series (OoT: Recompiled, .scaffold/upgrades/lighting-design.md).

The two headers patch 0002 declared are filled in. Every frame with ray tracing on builds one
bottom level structure per traced draw call from the world space positions the vertex processor
already writes and the draw call's own indices, into a pool that grows to the busiest frame,
and one top level structure over them with the renderer's own query masks and hit groups; the
output and history textures are made at the traced scene's size; the binding table, the lights
and the instance records rotate through a ring as deep as the workload queue so the CPU never
writes what the GPU is still reading; the descriptor sets of the compose, filter, luminance and
post process passes are bound to those textures.

The shader cache builds a pipeline over the raster path's four descriptor sets from one DXIL
library, src/shaders/Raytracing.hlsl, with the five ray generation entries the renderer
dispatches, two miss shaders and two hit groups. Only the primary ray does work: it walks the
structures (the instances that take part in depth; the sky box around the eye carries the
no-depth mask) and writes the hit distance, the world position, the geometric normal and the
instance, and hands the raster color of the pixel to the diffuse buffer for the passes that
come. The pinned composite lights that buffer and no pass writes a light yet, so the final view
is black at this phase; the depth view draws the scene as the rays see it, which is what this
phase proves, and phase 54 composites into the raster output. The build is traced once a second.

Three changes to the pinned renderer ride along, each marked "patch 0003" where it sits: the
projection pass runs whenever ray tracing is on, because the traced scene reads the transforms
only it fills; the binding table is built after the descriptor sets exist rather than before;
and the traced scene is the perspective projection with the most draw calls plus everything
sharing its matrices, because this game draws its sky first under a view of its own and the
first-perspective-projection heuristic traced four sky quads and rasterized the world.

Buffers that grow (a structure, a scratch) are retired for as many frames as the queue keeps in
flight rather than freed at once, and the top level structure is built even when the frame has
no instances, so the descriptor that names it is never left unwritten.

